name: web
boss: crawler
flavor: A site you own, on the loopback that only means this machine.
brief: These probe a practice website the lab stands up on 127.0.0.1 -- your
brief: own, and the only address in range. `httpx` asks a URL what it is;
brief: `gobuster` guesses hidden paths from a wordlist. Same tools the pros
brief: point at real targets, aimed here where it is safe to learn them.
tools: httpx gobuster ffuf nmap nuclei
lab: web
---
q: probe @weburl@ and {show its status code|report what status code it returns|find out what status code comes back}, with `httpx`
a: httpx -u @weburl@ -sc -silent -nc
out: 200
ref: httpx
teach: httpx
hint: -u the url, -sc for the status code, -silent -nc for clean output
---
q: {get the page title of|check what title is set on|read the html title tag from} @weburl@ with `httpx`
a: httpx -u @weburl@ -title -silent -nc
out: practice site
ref: httpx
teach: httpx
hint: -title asks for the <title> of the page
---
q: probe @weburl@ and {show the web server it runs|identify the server software behind it|find out what server powers it}, with `httpx`
a: httpx -u @weburl@ -server -silent -nc
out: [Ss]erver|[Pp]ython|http
ref: httpx
teach: httpx
hint: -server reports the Server header
---
q: {find the hidden page on|discover what secret page hides on|hunt down whatever page is hiding on} @weburl@ with `gobuster`, using ``words.txt``
a: gobuster dir -u @weburl@ -w words.txt -q
out: secret
ref: gobuster
teach: gobuster
hint: dir mode, -u the url, -w the wordlist, -q for quiet output
seed: printf 'admin\nsecret\nlogin\nhello\n' > words.txt
---
q: run `gobuster` dir on @weburl@ with ``words.txt``, {showing the status of each path|printing the status code beside every path it finds|revealing what status every found path returns}
a: gobuster dir -u @weburl@ -w words.txt
out: Status: 200
ref: gobuster
teach: gobuster
hint: without -q, gobuster prints the status of every path it finds
seed: printf 'admin\nsecret\nlogin\nhello\n' > words.txt
---
q: {fuzz for hidden paths on|brute-force hidden paths across|hunt down whatever paths are hiding on} @weburl@ with `ffuf`, using ``words.txt``, keeping only 200s
a: ffuf -u @weburl@/FUZZ -w words.txt -mc 200 -s
out: secret
ref: ffuf
teach: ffuf
hint: put ``FUZZ`` where the word goes in -u, -w the wordlist, -mc 200 keeps only hits, -s quiet
seed: mkdir -p .config/ffuf
seed: printf 'admin\nsecret\nlogin\nindex\n' > words.txt
---
q: {scan the host behind|find out whether a port is listening on|run a connect-mode scan of} @weburl@ for its open port with `nmap`, a TCP connect scan
a: nmap -sT -p @webport@ 127.0.0.1
a: nmap -sT 127.0.0.1
out: open
ref: nmap
teach: nmap
hint: -sT is the unprivileged connect scan; -p names the port to check
---
q: {run the default vulnerability checks against|check for known issues on|scan for common vulnerabilities on} @weburl@ with `nuclei`
a: nuclei -u @weburl@ -silent
a: nuclei -u @weburl@
ref: nuclei
teach: nuclei
hint: -u the target url; nuclei runs its template checks against it
